# Centrivical: Mini-Book and Companion App Package

This package has five parts:

1. **Publishing plan**: what goes where and what to decide first.
2. **Complete book manuscript**: a single Markdown file you can build into PDF and EPUB.
3. **Book build and Google Play Books upload steps.**
4. **Complete Android app source**: Kotlin with Jetpack Compose.
5. **APK and Play Store build and release steps**, plus a final checklist.

Throughout, the term keeps its **proposed** status. Neither the book nor the app claims the word is established.

---

## Part 1 — Publishing plan

| Item | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| **Book title** | *Centrivical: A Proposed Kinematic Term for Inward Swirling Flow* |
| **Book platform** | Google Play Books Partner Center. Books published there also appear in Google Books search. |
| **Formats** | Upload **both** a PDF and an EPUB. The PDF preserves the mathematics exactly. Check how the EPUB renders equations in the Partner Center preview. |
| **ISBN** | Optional. Google assigns an internal identifier (GGKEY) if you have none. Buy an ISBN only if you want listings beyond Google. Cost and source vary by country. |
| **License** | Consider **CC BY 4.0** for the text. A term proposal spreads more easily if people can quote and reuse it. |
| **Price** | Free is sensible for a proposal. A paid listing requires a payments profile. |
| **App name** | *Centrivical Explorer* (package `org.centrivical.explorer`) |
| **App features** | (1) Definition reader; (2) uncertainty-aware classifier with the transverse angle β; (3) interactive streamline plots of the canonical examples, including the L8c counterexample |
| **App distribution** | A signed **APK** works for direct download or sideloading. **Google Play requires an AAB**, not an APK, for new apps. |
| **Citability (optional)** | Deposit the PDF on Zenodo for a free DOI. Technical terms are adopted through citable documents more than through store listings. |

**Before publishing**, search Google Scholar, Google Books and arXiv for “centrivical.” This is only to confirm nobody uses the word differently. The book already disclaims priority.

